#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# DAFTAR — refuse (1) any push that DELETES a remote ref, and (2) any push
# whose destination is `main`.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, and what changed on 2026-11-03 (DR-83).
#
# The deny list in `.claude/settings.local.json` was measured, rule by rule,
# with negative controls that could not change anything if they passed. Nine of
# ten new rules refused as intended. One did not:
#
#     git push origin :dr83-nonexistent-branch      ->  NOT refused; it ran
#
# A variant naming the remote explicitly, `Bash(git push origin :*)`, also
# failed to fire. So ANY deny rule carrying `:` in that position is inert — the
# likely cause is that `:` is read as the `Tool(param:value)` separator and the
# rule never parses into what it looks like. Three pre-existing rules are in
# that same class and are therefore very probably inert too:
#
#     Bash(git push * :main)
#     Bash(git push * HEAD:main)
#     Bash(git push * HEAD:refs/heads/main*)
#
# They are deliberately LEFT IN PLACE (the owner's instruction), documented as
# ineffective. This hook is what actually refuses, because git enforces it on
# this machine whatever command or agent asked for the push — a hook does not
# depend on a pattern syntax parsing the way it reads.
#
# A CORRECTION TO THIS FILE'S OWN EARLIER COMMENT. The previous version stated
# that GitHub branch protection was UNAVAILABLE here, because the repository had
# gone private and the API answered `403 Upgrade to GitHub Pro`. That is no
# longer true, and it understated the protection. Measured on 2026-11-03:
#
#     allow_deletions      = false
#     allow_force_pushes   = false
#     enforce_admins       = true
#     required checks      = 6,  strict = true
#
# AND THAT COUNT IS AS OF THAT DATE, not a current reading: the cart carrying
# this file runs TEN jobs, so «6» describes 2026-11-03 and nothing else. It is
# kept here because a dated measurement is evidence; it was REMOVED from the
# refusal message below, where it read as present tense and was measured by
# nothing (third review of this cart, finding 7).
#
# So `main` cannot be deleted or force-pushed on the server either. The two
# controls are independent and both are wanted: the server refuses what reaches
# it, and this hook refuses before anything reaches the network at all — which
# is what makes a negative control for it safe to run.
#
# WHAT IT REFUSES
#   1. ANY DELETION, of ANY branch: a pre-push line whose LOCAL sha is forty
#      zeros is a deletion, whatever the ref is called. Branches are cheap to
#      recreate only when someone still knows the sha; a deletion here is
#      refused and must be done deliberately.
#   2. ANY PUSH WHOSE DESTINATION IS `main`, by any spelling — `main`,
#      `refs/heads/main`, `HEAD:main`, `anything:refs/heads/main` — and
#      regardless of the remote's name, because a wrong remote is one of the
#      ways this goes wrong.
#
# WHAT IT ALLOWS
#   Everything else: creating a branch, updating a branch, and the pull-request
#   merges the owner authorizes, which happen on GitHub's side and never reach
#   this hook.
#
# HOW TO SEE IT WORK (it refuses, and prints why, without touching the network):
#   git push origin :some-branch
#   git push origin HEAD:main
#
# IT IS NOW TRACKED, AND THAT IS A TRADE, NOT A PURE GAIN (owner's decision
# DR-180/3, 2026-11-24: «وهذا تشديد»).
#
# It used to live in `.git/hooks/`, which is not part of the repository: it did
# not travel with a clone, it protected one checkout, and — the part that
# mattered — NO PULL REQUEST COULD EDIT IT. Here, under `.githooks/` and
# selected by `core.hooksPath`, the FILE travels with every clone and every
# worktree and its changes are reviewable in a diff — but the SELECTION does
# not travel: `core.hooksPath` is local configuration that every clone must set
# for itself (`npm run setup:hooks`). The first wording of this paragraph said
# the arrangement travels, which is the half that is false, and the setup
# script's own header said the true thing three lines away. Review of this cart
# named the contradiction. What it loses is immunity: any
# branch that touches this file changes the guard, and a weakening would arrive
# through the same door as a strengthening.
#
# So the move is only a tightening if the guard is itself guarded, and two
# things do that:
#
#   * `tests-unit/pre-push-hook-law.test.ts` PINS the two refusals by content
#     AND RUNS THIS FILE end to end against a local bare repository — a push
#     aimed at `main` is refused, a deletion is refused, and an ordinary branch
#     push is allowed. The positive control is what makes the other two mean
#     something: a hook that refused everything would also "pass" the first two.
#   * `npm run setup:hooks` sets `core.hooksPath` and is idempotent, so a
#     checkout whose hooks were never wired is a measurable state rather than a
#     silent one.
#
# Nothing in that law touches the network: the remote is a bare repository in a
# temporary directory, so the refusal is exercised for real with nothing at
# stake.
#
# `git push --no-verify` bypasses it. That is deliberate — a refusal should be
# overridable on purpose, never by accident — and bypassing it is forbidden by
# the charter (§7) rather than by this file: NEEDING the flag is a stop
# condition, and measuring afterwards that nothing was skipped is not
# permission for it.

set -u

protected='main'
blocked=0
# THE DELETION SENTINEL IS THE NULL OBJECT ID, AND ITS WIDTH IS THE
# REPOSITORY'S. git sends all-zeros as the local sha of a delete: 40 hex zeros
# under SHA-1, 64 under SHA-256 (`git rev-parse --show-object-format`).
# Measured on review of this cart: a deletion line carrying 64 zeros was
# ALLOWED, because the test compared against a literal forty. This repository
# is sha1 today so nothing was exposed — but the header above claims «ANY
# DELETION, of ANY branch», and a claim that wide must not rest on a width.
# The test is now «all zeros, at least one of them», which holds for every
# hash git may use.

# git feeds pre-push one line per ref on stdin:
#   <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>
while read -r local_ref local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do
  [ -z "${remote_ref:-}" ] && continue

  # Normalise `main` and `refs/heads/main` to the same name.
  short_remote="${remote_ref#refs/heads/}"

  # (1) A deletion of ANY ref — the local sha is the null object id, i.e. all
  #     zeros, whatever the repository's hash width.
  case "${local_sha:-}" in
    '' | *[!0]*) ;;
    *)
      blocked=1
      echo "pre-push: REFUSED — DELETION of the remote ref '$short_remote'." >&2
      echo "          local ref:  ${local_ref:-<none, this is a delete>}" >&2
      echo "          remote ref: $remote_ref" >&2
      echo "          local sha:  $local_sha  (the null object id = delete)" >&2
      continue
      ;;
  esac

  # (2) Any push whose destination is the protected branch.
  if [ "$short_remote" = "$protected" ]; then
    blocked=1
    echo "pre-push: REFUSED — push to the protected branch '$protected'." >&2
    echo "          local ref:  ${local_ref:-<none>}" >&2
    echo "          remote ref: $remote_ref" >&2
  fi
done

if [ "$blocked" -ne 0 ]; then
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  Two things this hook refuses, and why:

    * DELETING a remote ref, any ref. Recreating it needs someone to still
      know the sha. If you mean it, mean it explicitly.

    * PUSHING TO `main`. `main` is changed by merging a pull request, never by
      pushing to it.

  GitHub also refuses both for `main` (allow_deletions=false,
  allow_force_pushes=false, enforce_admins=true, and a set of required checks
  this message does not count). This hook refuses BEFORE the network, so
  nothing reaches origin to be refused.

  If you genuinely mean to do either, that is an owner decision:
  `git push --no-verify` bypasses this hook. Say so, rather than letting it
  happen by accident.

MSG
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
